# Parse, validate and encode Thing Descriptions and Thing Models

## Goal

Parse a checked-in W3C WoT Thing Description (TD) and a small Thing Model
(TM) with the core `wotex` package, validate them against the pinned TD 1.1
schema, encode them canonically, read them back unchanged, and see the
structured error every malformed input produces. Nothing here contacts a
device, a network or a broker: a TD is data, and this notebook only ever
turns bytes into values and values back into bytes.

## Prerequisites

```elixir
Mix.install([
  {:wotex_lab, "~> 0.1.0"}
])
```

The requirement above is a published-artifact requirement. At the time this
notebook was written none of the `wotex*` packages is published on Hex; the
notebook is verified against the workspace source cohort recorded in
`priv/provenance/source-cohort.json` by `test/wotex/lab/cookbook_test.exs`,
which evaluates every Elixir cell below in order (skipping this install
cell). `Mix.install/1` never points at a Git checkout; when the packages
ship, this cell installs the same public API the tests exercised.

## Package and consumer ownership

| Concern | Owner | Where |
| --- | --- | --- |
| TD/TM parsing, schema validation, canonical encoding, error shape | `wotex` (package-implements) | `Wotex.ThingDescription`, `Wotex.ThingModel`, `Wotex.Error` |
| The fixture document and its manifest | `wotex_lab` (consumer) | `priv/fixtures/thermal/` |
| Telemetry around the parse | `wotex_lab` (consumer) | `Wotex.Lab.Telemetry.span/4` |

The Lab adds no WoT semantics. The only Lab call in the run cells is the
telemetry span; every other call is the package API a consumer would use.

## Run

Start by capturing Lab telemetry into this process so the safe-telemetry
section can inspect what a parse emits.

```elixir
alias Wotex.{ThingDescription, ThingModel}
alias Wotex.Lab.Telemetry

telemetry_id = "cookbook-parse-td-#{System.unique_integer([:positive])}"

events =
  for component <- Telemetry.components(),
      operation <- Telemetry.operations(),
      event <- [:start, :stop, :exception],
      do: [:wotex, :lab, component, operation, event]

:ok = :telemetry.attach_many(telemetry_id, events, &Telemetry.forward/4, self())
```

Read the fixture and parse it. `ThingDescription.parse/1` decodes JSON,
validates the document against the pinned schema and keeps the source bytes.

```elixir
path = Application.app_dir(:wotex_lab, "priv/fixtures/thermal/thing-description.json")
json = File.read!(path)

{:ok, td} =
  Telemetry.span(:scenario, :parse, %{profile: :cookbook}, fn -> ThingDescription.parse(json) end)

{ThingDescription.id(td), ThingDescription.to_map(td)["properties"] |> Map.keys()}
```

Validation is a separate, explicit call; a parsed TD is already valid, so
`validate/1` returns it unchanged.

```elixir
{:ok, ^td} = ThingDescription.validate(td)
{:ok, canonical} = ThingDescription.encode(td, :canonical)
{:ok, reparsed} = ThingDescription.parse(canonical)
{:ok, source} = ThingDescription.encode(td, :source)

%{
  canonical_bytes: byte_size(canonical),
  source_bytes: byte_size(source),
  roundtrip_equal?: ThingDescription.to_map(reparsed) == ThingDescription.to_map(td)
}
```

A Thing Model is built the same way from a map and encodes canonically too.

```elixir
model_map = %{
  "@context" => Wotex.td_context_1_1(),
  "@type" => "tm:ThingModel",
  "title" => "Room model",
  "properties" => %{"temperature" => %{"type" => "number", "unit" => "Cel"}}
}

{:ok, model} = ThingModel.from_map(model_map)
{:ok, ^model} = ThingModel.validate(model)
{:ok, model_json} = ThingModel.encode(model, :canonical)
{:ok, model_again} = ThingModel.parse(model_json)
tm_roundtrip? = ThingModel.to_map(model_again) == model_map
```

## Deliberate breakage

Every failure is a `Wotex.Error` (or a list of them) with a stable `code`,
`phase` and JSON pointer `path`; the message never echoes the input.

```elixir
{:error, invalid_json} = ThingDescription.parse("{")
{:error, non_object} = ThingDescription.parse("[]")
{:error, violations} = ThingDescription.from_map(%{"@context" => Wotex.td_context_1_1()})
{:error, model_non_object} = ThingModel.from_map("not-an-object")

%{
  invalid_json: {invalid_json.code, invalid_json.phase, invalid_json.path},
  non_object: {non_object.code, non_object.phase},
  missing_fields: Enum.map(violations, &{&1.code, &1.path}),
  model_non_object: model_non_object.code
}
```

## Safe telemetry

The span closed with an allowlisted outcome and a profile label. No document,
identifier or byte count of the TD became a label.

```elixir
drain = fn drain ->
  receive do
    {:wotex_lab_telemetry, event, measurements, metadata} ->
      [{event, measurements, metadata} | drain.(drain)]
  after
    0 -> []
  end
end

spans = drain.(drain)
:telemetry.detach(telemetry_id)

parse_stop =
  Enum.find(spans, fn {event, _measurements, _metadata} ->
    event == [:wotex, :lab, :scenario, :parse, :stop]
  end)

{_event, measurements, metadata} = parse_stop
labels_safe? = metadata == %{profile: :cookbook, outcome: :ok}
{Telemetry.to_milliseconds(measurements.duration), metadata, labels_safe?}
```

## Expected output

| Check | Expectation |
| --- | --- |
| `td-id` | The fixture identifies `urn:wotex:lab:thermostat:1` |
| `validate-ok` | A parsed TD validates unchanged |
| `canonical-roundtrip` | Canonical bytes parse back to the same document |
| `tm-roundtrip` | The Thing Model encodes and parses back to its map |
| `invalid-json-error` | `{"` fails with `:invalid_json` in phase `:parse` at `/` |
| `schema-violation-path` | A missing title is `:schema_violation` at `/title` |
| `non-object-refused` | Arrays and strings fail with `:object_required` |

The final cell returns these checks as a map; the automated runner asserts
every value is `true`.

```elixir
%{
  "td-id" => ThingDescription.id(td) == "urn:wotex:lab:thermostat:1",
  "validate-ok" => match?({:ok, ^td}, ThingDescription.validate(td)),
  "canonical-roundtrip" => ThingDescription.to_map(reparsed) == ThingDescription.to_map(td),
  "tm-roundtrip" => tm_roundtrip?,
  "invalid-json-error" =>
    invalid_json.code == :invalid_json and invalid_json.phase == :parse and
      invalid_json.path == "/",
  "schema-violation-path" =>
    Enum.any?(violations, &(&1.code == :schema_violation and &1.path == "/title")),
  "non-object-refused" =>
    non_object.code == :object_required and model_non_object.code == :object_required and
      labels_safe?
}
```

## Public seam

`core.td` — `Wotex.ThingDescription.parse/2`, `from_map/2`, `to_map/1` and
`encode/2` (package-implements). The seam is recorded with its revision
pinned source URL in `priv/provenance/source-index.json`.

## Spec and completion IDs

* Lab: WLB.03 (`WLB-C03`, `WLB-C08`).
* Upstream consumer evidence: `WTX-C01`, `WTX-C02`, `WTX-C03`, `WTX-C04`
  (`wotex:WTX.01`, `WTX.03`, `WTX.04`). Lab evidence does not close those
  items; only the `wotex` owner can.

## Replacement adapter

There is nothing to replace here: parsing is package-owned. A consumer that
needs a different validation profile passes options to `parse/2` or
`from_map/2` rather than wrapping the result. To use another JSON codec,
decode yourself and call `from_map/2`; the value semantics do not change.
